{
  "concept": "For a projectile launched and landing at the same height, horizontal range depends on launch speed squared and on sin(2θ) — so range grows quadratically with v₀ at fixed angle, peaks at 45°, and is identical for complementary angles that reach very different heights.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal ground line serving as the y = 0 baseline for both panels",
    "Common launch point at the left end of the ground line (origin)",
    "Small axis key at upper right of panel (a): y arrow up, x arrow right",
    "Panel (a) label and panel (b) label beneath their respective plots",
    "Panel (a): three parabolic trajectories from the same origin, orange (30 m/s), purple (40 m/s), teal (50 m/s), all at 45°",
    "Panel (a): three color-matched velocity arrows at the origin labeled '30 m/s', '40 m/s', '50 m/s'",
    "Panel (a): angle arc between the ground line and the launch arrows labeled '45°'",
    "Panel (a): dashed vertical guide lines dropping from each trajectory apex to the ground line",
    "Panel (a): three horizontal dimension bars with end ticks below the ground line labeled 'R = 91.8 m', 'R = 163 m', 'R = 255 m'",
    "Panel (b): three parabolic trajectories from the same origin at fixed speed, orange (15°), purple (45°), teal (75°)",
    "Panel (b): three color-matched velocity arrows at the origin with nested angle arcs labeled '15°', '45°', '75°'",
    "Panel (b): label 'v₀ = 50 m/s' near the launch arrows",
    "Panel (b): dashed vertical lines at the shared landing point of the 15° and 75° curves and at the apex of the 75° curve",
    "Panel (b): two dimension bars labeled 'R = 128 m' and 'R = 255 m'",
    "Live readout panel showing current v₀, θ, computed range R = v₀²sin(2θ)/g, apex height, and time of flight",
    "Highlighted active trajectory with a moving landing-point marker and its own range bar"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "panelView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Only one launch parameter is varied at a time: panel (a) isolates speed, panel (b) isolates angle.",
      "label": "Panel",
      "options": ["(a) vary speed, θ = 45°", "(b) vary angle, v₀ = 50 m/s"],
      "default": "(a) vary speed, θ = 45°"
    },
    {
      "id": "initialSpeed",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "At a fixed 45° launch, range stretches with the square of initial speed — doubling v₀ quadruples R, so the textbook's 30/40/50 m/s curves land at 91.8, 163, and 255 m.",
      "label": "Initial speed v₀ (m/s)",
      "range": [10, 60, 1],
      "default": 30
    },
    {
      "id": "launchAngle",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "At fixed speed, the parabola trades height for reach; range follows sin(2θ) and is maximal at 45°.",
      "label": "Launch angle θ (degrees)",
      "range": [5, 85, 1],
      "default": 45
    },
    {
      "id": "showComplement",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Complementary angles (θ and 90° − θ) land on exactly the same spot but follow very different arcs and flight times.",
      "label": "Show complementary angle (90° − θ)",
      "default": false
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "One launch, one parabola",
      "narration": "A projectile leaves the origin at 30 m/s and 45° and returns to the same ground line 91.8 m away. The dashed line marks its apex; the bar below measures its range.",
      "state": { "panelView": "(a) vary speed, θ = 45°", "initialSpeed": 30, "launchAngle": 45, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "More speed, much more range",
      "narration": "Raise v₀ to 40 m/s: the angle is unchanged, but the range jumps to 163 m — far more than the 33% speed increase would suggest.",
      "state": { "panelView": "(a) vary speed, θ = 45°", "initialSpeed": 40, "launchAngle": 45, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Range scales as v₀²",
      "narration": "At 50 m/s the projectile reaches 255 m. Comparing 30, 40 and 50 m/s shows the ranges scale like 9 : 16 : 25 — exactly the squares of the speeds.",
      "state": { "panelView": "(a) vary speed, θ = 45°", "initialSpeed": 50, "launchAngle": 45, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Now hold speed, sweep the angle",
      "narration": "Switch to panel (b): v₀ is locked at 50 m/s. A shallow 15° launch gives a flat arc that lands at 128 m.",
      "state": { "panelView": "(b) vary angle, v₀ = 50 m/s", "initialSpeed": 50, "launchAngle": 15, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "45° maximizes the range",
      "narration": "Drag θ upward: the range grows to a maximum of 255 m at 45°, then falls again as the arc becomes steep and tall.",
      "state": { "panelView": "(b) vary angle, v₀ = 50 m/s", "initialSpeed": 50, "launchAngle": 45, "showComplement": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Complementary angles share a landing point",
      "narration": "At 75° the projectile climbs much higher and hangs much longer, yet it lands at the very same 128 m as the 15° shot — complementary angles give equal range.",
      "state": { "panelView": "(b) vary angle, v₀ = 50 m/s", "initialSpeed": 50, "launchAngle": 75, "showComplement": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Range obeys R = v₀²sin(2θ)/g: speed enters squared (so it dominates), while the angle enters only through sin(2θ), which peaks at 45° and takes the same value for θ and 90° − θ.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two stacked panels sharing a visual language, each with a single horizontal ground line and a common launch point at the far left. Curves are smooth parabolas that start and end on the ground line, drawn in the textbook's three colors: orange (slowest / shallowest), purple (middle), teal (fastest / steepest). Short color-matched velocity arrows fan out from the origin with text labels ('30 m/s', '40 m/s', '50 m/s' in panel a; 'v₀ = 50 m/s' with nested arcs '15°', '45°', '75°' in panel b); panel (a) has a single arc labeled '45°' since all three share the angle. Thin gray dashed verticals drop from each apex (panel a) and from the shared 15°/75° landing point and the tall 75° apex (panel b). Below the ground line, stacked horizontal dimension bars with arrowheads and end ticks carry centered labels 'R = 91.8 m', 'R = 163 m', 'R = 255 m' in panel (a) and 'R = 128 m', 'R = 255 m' in panel (b); bar lengths must match the landing x-positions of their curves. A small y-up / x-right axis key sits at the upper right of panel (a); italic '(a)' and '(b)' captions sit centered beneath each panel. In the interactive version the textbook curves remain as faint reference ghosts while the learner-controlled trajectory is drawn bold with a filled landing dot, a live apex dashed line, and its own dimension bar that stretches in real time; a compact readout shows v₀, θ, R, max height and flight time. Both panels use the same horizontal scale so a 255 m range is visually identical across panels."
}

